@src 56:123
@pyq U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Pre) 2017
Which one of the following substances is a herbicide?
(a) Chlorpyrifos
(b) Carbendazim
(c) Quinolphos
(d) Butachlor
@ans (d)
@end

**2,4-D** (a synthetic auxin) kills broad-leaved weeds in cereals and paddy; **butachlor** is a pre-emergence paddy herbicide. **Chlorpyrifos** and **quinalphos** are insecticides and **carbendazim** a fungicide. Herbicides are selective because weeds and crops absorb and break them down at **different rates**.

@q 56:98-99

@q 56:106-108

The **largest flower** is ***Rafflesia arnoldii*** (Sumatra, up to 1 m). Seeds need **water, oxygen and the right temperature** to germinate, but usually **not light**. Mustard oil is often adulterated with **argemone** seeds (the source keys poppy, as argemone is the "prickly poppy"). **Mustard gas** was used in **World War I**. Wax coating extends the **storage life** of fruits.

@q 56:109-113

**Clay** holds the most water because its tiny particles leave **many very small pores**; the Reason (large pores) is false. **Eutrophication** is nutrient enrichment of water (from fertiliser and sewage) causing algal blooms that **deplete oxygen**. Wool decomposes; plastic, glass and aluminium do not. An **auxanometer** measures plant growth, and **annual rings** give a tree's age (*dendrochronology*).

@q 56:118-120

The **Green Revolution** (from 1966-67) used **high-yielding dwarf varieties** that needed **more fertiliser and more water**. Waterlogged **paddy fields release methane**. The **Dapog** rice nursery method came from the **Philippines**. The **Destructive Insects and Pests Act** (1914) still governs plant quarantine in India.

